+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-restaurants-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must include a public `github.com` repository URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS listings unless the repository itself is the open source product being listed.
+- The project must be relevant to restaurant workflows: guest support, ordering, menu OCR, forecasting, recommendation, search, voice, or operations.
+- Include the repository's star count, primary language, license, and recent last-commit date from GitHub.
+- Add a short note explaining why the tool is useful for restaurants.
+- If a category has fewer than 5 strong repos, document the gap instead of adding weak matches.
+
+## Preferred Entry Format
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary based on the GitHub README. Include stars, language, license, and last commit date.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** 123 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-31
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific restaurant use case
+```
+
+## Process
+
+1. Fork the repository.
+2. Add or update entries in `README.md`.
+3. Keep descriptions concise, factual, and based on the repository README.
+4. Open a pull request with a short explanation of what you added and why it fits this list.
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer self-hosted or clearly open source tools.
+- Prefer active repositories over abandoned experiments.
+- Do not add tools that only have a landing page, app store listing, or closed vendor signup flow.
+- Do not remove honest gap notes just to increase the repo count.
